{
 "__about__": "The NovaStar COEX enterprise SNMP arc (1.3.6.1.4.1.319) as OBSERVED on one MX30 -- firmware V1.5.1, read over SNMP itself (CONTROLLER_FIRMWARE, and the input- and output-card firmware OIDs) -- on 2026-09-26, with VMP closed: one snmpwalk, SNMPv2c, community public, the only community ever sent. Every one of the 170 varbinds the walk returned is here, in walk order, as net-snmp decoded it: {\"type\": \"INTEGER\" | \"STRING\" | \"Counter64\", \"value\": ...}. Types: 102 INTEGER, 44 STRING, 24 Counter64. Two of the STRINGs, INPUT_CARD_NAME (.10.10.20.3.1.2) and OUTPUT_CARD_NAME (.10.10.30.3.1.2), are empty; net-snmp printed them as = \"\" with no type tag, which is how it renders a zero-length OCTET STRING. INTEGER values are JSON numbers. Counter64 values are exact decimal STRINGS, because all but one of them are 64-bit masks above 2**53 (for example 18446744073709551614 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) and a JSON number would lose their low bits in JavaScript -- the bits that carry the per-port and per-card status. How the controller BER-encoded those Counter64 values on the wire (8 bytes, or 9 with a leading 0x00) is UNKNOWN: snmpwalk text decodes either, and nothing was captured. The walk ended with endOfMibView after .10.200.6, so nothing past the last OID here was served; the gaps are real too (.10.10.20.4.1.2, .10.10.30.4.1.2 and .10.20.1.2.1.8 were not served). The MIB-2 system group (1.3.6.1.2.1.1) was not served (OBSERVED: a walk of it returned nothing, and a v2c GET of sysDescr drew noSuchName); other MIB-2 groups were not walked (UNKNOWN). SNMP was off on the unit; it was enabled for the walk by an HTTP PUT of /api/v1/device/snmpstate with {\"state\": true} and disabled straight after with {\"state\": false}, and read back false (a PUT with {\"value\": true} had answered Success and left it reading false). Real structure and numbers throughout: the Counter64 masks, the \"ERROR: ...\" strings in place of data and their trailing space, the trailing space on each INPUT_SOURCE_TYPE, the undocumented subtrees and the undocumented port-table values under .10.10.30.5.1.4.Y. SYNTHETIC values replace every identifier and the clock: CONTROLLER_TIME (.10.10.1.1) is 2000-01-01 00:00:00 -- the format YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS is OBSERVED, the value is not; the controller name (.10.10.1.4) is \"Stage left\", the same synthetic label as tests/fixtures/mx30_like_api.json's monitor/info name, because the two were recorded as equal on the unit (a comparison made before masking, which the masked evidence cannot re-check); the screen name (.10.20.1.2.1.1, undocumented) is \"Main\", that fixture's synthetic screenName; the three serials (.10.10.1.6, .10.10.20.3.1.4, .10.10.30.3.1.4) are FIXTURE-SN-*, and whether the real three were equal is UNKNOWN, so do not join on them; the MAC is from the RFC 7042 documentation range and the IP from RFC 5737 TEST-NET-1. The format of a synthetic identifier is not evidence of the real one's format. Readings, with labels (see src/novasun/snmp.py and docs/read-only-monitoring.md for the full account): model MX30 and firmware V1.5.1 OBSERVED; CONTROLLER_ROLE 1 OBSERVED on a unit that drove the wall alone, so its meaning is UNKNOWN and it must not be displayed as \"backup\"; reading TEMPERATURE_POINT_VALUE 3100, VOLTAGE_POINT_VALUE 1156 and the two frame rates 5000 as x100 (31.00 C, 11.56 V, 50.00 Hz) is REASONED, the voltage matching HTTP mainBoardVoltage exactly and the temperature only approximately; SCREEN_BRIGHTNESS \"50.0\" as percent REASONED; .10.10.10.6.N.3 as fan rpm REASONED; OUTPUT_SLOT_STATUS, INPUT_SLOT_STATUS and ETHERNET_PORT_STATUS are Counter64 masks where the document gives 0/1 (OBSERVED), read as one bit per slot or port, 0 = connected/normal, least significant bit = index 1 (REASONED) -- ETHERNET_PORT_STATUS clears ports 1-5, the five linked outputs, though cards were online on 1, 3 and 5 only, so it reads as link state rather than cabinet presence (REASONED); RECEIVING_CARDS_ONLINE (.10.10.30.5.1.4.Y.1) is INTEGER 24 on ports 1, 3 and 5 and the STRING \"ERROR: there are no cabinets in port \" on the other seven (OBSERVED), and its sum, 72, equals the undocumented Counter64 .10.10.30.4.1.5 (read as the total receiving-card count, REASONED); the documented per-card receiving-card status forms .10.10.30.6.N.1.Y.{1,2}.M were absent from the walk, and in their place .10.10.30.6.1.1.Y.1 and .Y.2 are Counter64 masks, 0xFFFFFFFFFF000000 on ports 1, 3 and 5 and all ones on the other seven (OBSERVED), read as one bit per receiving card with unused bits set (REASONED), bit order within the 24 UNKNOWN; strings beginning \"ERROR:\" (nine of them) are best treated as absent (REASONED). Any other undocumented OID here is an OBSERVED value whose meaning is UNKNOWN or at most REASONED. Nothing here says what another MX30, another model or another firmware does. Built by hand from the masked walk; tests/test_snmp_fixture.py pins its structure and asserts no show data is in it.",
